With Battersea Power Station’s future once again in doubt, its new owner might wish it could add Cedric Price’s Bathat scheme to the list of options for the site.

&nbsp;

Price argued that trying to preserve it was the wrong approach. In 1984, when its owner held a limited architectural competition for the site, he launched his counterproposal in BD. This involved removing the lateral brick walls — the source of most maintenance problems — while supporting the upper levels and four chimneys on the existing steel structure. He pointed out that a signifi-cant part of the building had little historical value and was not worth the huge conser-vation costs, but by keeping the control room and chimneys as a celebrated symbol of London’s skyline, the land could be be released for redevelopment. He was deliberately vague on what could go on the site, believing an architect’s role was only to widen options not narrow them.

